      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We start by analyzing the given diagram. We have a triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) with angle \\( \\angle BAD = 93^\\circ \\) and angle \\( \\angle ADB = 130^\\circ \\).\n\nFirst, we determine the measure of angle \\( \\angle CAD \\):\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = \\angle ADB - \\angle BAD = 130^\\circ - 93^\\circ = 37^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nNext, since \\( \\angle BAC \\) is an internal angle of \\( \\triangle ABC \\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = \\angle BAD - \\angle CAD = 93^\\circ - 37^\\circ = 56^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the measure of angle \\( \\angle ACB \\). We know that the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, in \\( \\triangle ABC \\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BAC + \\angle ABC + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ.\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values, we get:\n\\[\n56^\\circ + \\angle ABC + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ.\n\\]\nSolving for \\( \\angle ABC + \\angle ACB \\), we obtain:\n\\[\n\\angle ABC + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 56^\\circ = 124^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( \\angle ACB \\), we need more information about \\( \\angle ABC \\). However, the problem does not provide any additional angles or side lengths. Therefore, we can only use the information given in the diagram to conclude that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]\n\nis the measure of \\( \\angle ACB \\) in degrees, assuming the diagram is perfect and no additional angles are given.",
